## Refactored with Async/Await

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// Basic usage
const user = await getUser(1);

// With error handling
try {
  const user = await getUser(1);
  console.log(user);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch user:', err.message);
}
```

---

### If `db.query` Doesn't Natively Support Promises

Some older database libraries (e.g., `mysql`) use callbacks only. Wrap them with `util.promisify` or a manual Promise:

**Option 1 — `util.promisify` (simplest)**
```javascript
const { promisify } = require('util');
const query = promisify(db.query).bind(db);

async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

**Option 2 — Manual Promise wrapper (most explicit)**
```javascript
function queryAsync(sql, params) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    db.query(sql, params, (err, rows) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else resolve(rows);
    });
  });
}

async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await queryAsync('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

---

### Key Differences

| | Callback Version | Async/Await Version |
|---|---|---|
| **Errors** | Passed to `callback(err)` | Thrown as exceptions |
| **Results** | Passed to `callback(null, result)` | Returned with `return` |
| **Error handling** | Manual `if (err)` checks | `try/catch` blocks |
| **Composability** | Nesting / pyramid of doom | Flat, readable flow |

> **Tip:** If you're starting a new project, prefer a library that supports Promises natively (e.g., `mysql2/promise`, `pg` with `pg-promise`, or an ORM like Prisma/Sequelize).
